    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A federation of Greek cities with shared citizenship, coinage and foreign policy, led by an annually elected general.</li><li>Under Aratus and Philopoemen it grew from four Achaean towns to the whole Peloponnese by 191 BCE.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rome">Rome</a> dissolved it after the war of 146 BCE and the sack of Corinth; Polybius made it a model for later federal thinkers.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>James Madison discussed the Achaean League in The Federalist No. 18 as a lesson for the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a> on the strengths and dangers of federal union.</p></aside>
    </section>

